            <ArticleTitle>Congenital cholesteatoma localized to the mastoid cavity and presenting as a mastoid abscess.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>305494</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1155/2015/305494</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Introduction. Congenital cholesteatoma is a pearly white mass that rarely originates from the mastoid process. Case Report. A 21-year-old male patient presented to our department with severe right mastoid pain and postauricular fluctuant swelling for 23 days. There was no preceding history of ear complaints and examination showed a normal right ear drum. Emergency exploration of the mastoid process was done on the same day and revealed localized cholesteatoma limited only to the mastoid cavity. Conclusion. Despite a rarity, the mastoid process should be always put in mind as a site of origin for congenital cholesteatoma.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
